      "subgoal": "Explain where interface and interaction design fits in the interactive system development lifecycle and why code design is not the focus of this lecture."
    },
    {
      "start": 25,
      "subgoal": "Describe the basic design questions and the starting points for interface and interaction design, including creativity, intuition, domain knowledge, and guidelines."
    },
    {
      "start": 59,
      "subgoal": "Explain that design representation is a form of design specification and that prototypes can serve as a design language."
    },
    {
      "start": 71,
      "subgoal": "Distinguish specific from generic design guidelines and introduce Shneiderman's Eight Golden Rules as a generic guideline set."
    },
    {
      "start": 87,
      "subgoal": "Explain Shneiderman's Eight Golden Rules and their possible applicability to extended reality interface design."
    },
    {
      "start": 200,
      "subgoal": "Identify the importance of XR interaction techniques and the three broad categories of interaction in immersive environments."
    },
    {
      "start": 212,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture and preview the continuation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "I merged the short design-language segment (original 59–70) with the preceding basic-concepts segment because together they cover the two introductory design questions: where to start and how to express/represent a design. I split the long Shneiderman rules segment (original 87–199) into four thematic rule groups, cutting at the rule announcements at sentences 123, 145, and 164, so each segment explains a closed group of related rules rather than all eight rules at once. I merged the short interaction-techniques segment (original 200–211) with the closing summary because it is only a brief transition/preview and, together with the summary, forms the lecture's final wrap-up. The other boundaries were kept because each segment starts at its own bridge and closes a sub-goal.",
